by mpdude at 2026-01-02T18:55:31Z

I had lost sight of this, but would like to see if I can get it finished with a bit of new year's energy ✨ .

I see two areas where we'd need to reach consensus.

#### Attribute merging

The examples above show that there are more attributes than just `@class` where we'd need to concatenate multiple values to build lists.

IMHO we need to design this in a way that we need not keep a list of "special" attributes in the code. There are too many standards/sources where these "special" attributes may come from, so we will never catch up. My suggestion would be to hand this problem to users in the following way:

* As long as scalar values are used, `html_attr_merge` will have latter values override former ones.
* For iterable values, perform `array_merge`-style merging, i. e. latter keys override former ones, numerical indices will be appended

Examples of how it could be used in practice:

* Plain `class` list: `html_attr_merge( { class: ['foo', 'bar'] }, { class: ['baz'] } )` => `{ class: ['foo', 'bar', 'baz'] }`
* `class` with named keys for semantic merging: `html_attr_merge( { class: ['foo'] }, { class: { importance: 'low' } }, { class: { importance: 'high' } })` => `{ class: ['foo', importance: 'high'] }`

Basically, it means that users need to know about the semantics of a given attribute and be disciplined about the values they use in attribute arrays.

As long as they use arrays consistently for attributes like e. g. `aria-describedby` or `class`, the use cases outlined in the discussion above are possible. For attributes like `id`, `href`, `checked`... arrays should never be used.

Regarding the case that non-scalar values meet with scalars, right now I'll cast both to array. We could also throw in that case.

If you have any better ideas, I'd be interested to know.

The following may lead to hard-to-spot errors:

`html_attr_merge( { class: 'foo' }, { class: 'bar' }, { class: ['baz'] } )` => `{ class: ['bar', 'baz'] }`

Keep in mind that the values for merging may come from different places (eg. passed as an argument to a macro), so you may not directly see that different value types are used.

"Flattening" of arrays into dedicated attributes happens only for `data` and `aria` keys, and happens right away while merging:

* `html_attr_merge( { data: { foo: 'bar', bar: 'baz' } } )` => `{ 'data-foo': 'bar', 'data-bar': 'baz' }`
* `html_attr_merge( { aria: { labelledby: ['id1', 'id2'] } } )` => `{ 'aria-labelledby': ['id1', 'id2'] }`

#### Printing attributes

Major discussion item: Handling of `''`, `null`, `true`, `false` and non-scalar values.

My suggestion:

* For `aria-*`, convert `true` to `'true'` and `false` to `'false'`.
* For `data-*`
  * JSON-encode non-scalars
  * otherwise, leave values alone
  * print all `data-*` attributes _except_ those with `null` values
* In general,
  * print `{ attr: '' }` as `attr=""`. You can e.g. use `a[title]` as a CSS selector to match `a` tags with `title` attributes, and the only way to create `<a title="">` would be `<a {{html_attr({ title: '' })}}>`. An explicit empty `alt` as in `<img ... alt=""/>` may be necessary to mark an image as decorative for assistive software, so we should be able to support this case.
  * omit attributes with `false` and `null` values
  * print `name="name"` for `true` values
  * for scalar values, use the space-separated concatenation of all values.

For `style`, non-numeric keys are used to create inline CSS, but that is probably not debatable.

WDYT?

